1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,177
A song comes on. Nothing is touching you, yet your arms rise.

2
00:00:04,177 --> 00:00:06,901
Chills. Goosebumps. A shiver down the spine.

3
00:00:06,901 --> 00:00:11,854
Your body is reacting to vibrations in the air as if they were physical contact.

4
00:00:11,854 --> 00:00:16,188
Only about half of people experience this. Scientists call it frisson.

5
00:00:16,788 --> 00:00:20,510
Frisson isn't in your ears. It's in your prediction system.

6
00:00:20,510 --> 00:00:23,601
Music works by making promises and delaying them.

7
00:00:23,601 --> 00:00:30,415
When the drop finally lands, or the key change lifts everything, your brain's reward circuit fires dopamine.

8
00:00:30,415 --> 00:00:33,002
The chills are literally a reward signal.

9
00:00:33,002 --> 00:00:38,113
Brain scans show the same circuit lighting up as when you eat sugar or win money.

10
00:00:38,713 --> 00:00:47,363
The triggers are surprisingly consistent: sudden volume, soaring voices, harmony surprises, the moment music almost stops and then explodes.

11
00:00:47,363 --> 00:00:49,711
In other words: violated expectations.

12
00:00:49,711 --> 00:00:55,210
And here's the twist: people who get more frisson score higher in openness to experience.

13
00:00:55,210 --> 00:01:01,450
Their brains don't just hear music. They simulate it, predicting every note, feeling every deviation.

14
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:04,614
There's also a colder, older explanation.

15
00:01:04,614 --> 00:01:08,116
Goosebumps are a reflex from when our ancestors had fur.

16
00:01:08,116 --> 00:01:12,431
Cold or threat made the hair stand up, to look bigger or trap warmth.

17
00:01:12,431 --> 00:01:17,497
When a song moves you, that ancient reflex misfires: emotion becomes temperature.

18
00:01:17,497 --> 00:01:22,250
Your chills are millions of years old, reacting to a song written last year.

19
00:01:22,850 --> 00:01:26,612
So the next time a song gives you chills, don't just enjoy it.

20
00:01:26,612 --> 00:01:30,616
Notice where it happens. That moment is a map of your predictions.

21
00:01:30,616 --> 00:01:34,499
Your body keeps a diary of what it loves, written in goosebumps.

22
00:01:34,499 --> 00:01:38,200
The question is: when did you last let a song do that to you?
